import EpistemicLedger.AuditRules

/-
Blind record for "Proving and Improving the Platonic Representation Hypothesis
with JEPAs" (Balestriero and LeCun, arXiv:2610.39302v1), read from
`paper/paper.txt` only.

The record says what each quoted claim leaned on in the paper's own derivation
or experiment, and what the quoted sentence itself carries. It does not say
whether a claim is true.

Reading conventions used here:
* `declared` and `declaredBets` are read off the QUOTED SENTENCE only. Caveats
  that the paper states elsewhere (e.g. its "Known limitations" paragraph in
  Section 7) are used as evidence for `used` and for hypothesis statuses, but do
  not count as declared by the sentence.
* For Theorem 1 "as stated" (claim 2), the standing definitions of the symbols
  it uses (Section 2.1, "World, modalities, and positive pairs") count as part
  of its wording.
* Modelling idealisations chosen by the authors are tagged `setByExperimenter`.
-/
namespace EpistemicLedger.Cases.BlindA.BalestrieroLeCun

inductive Dep where
  /-- The objective family the theory is about. Section 2.1, eq. (1): "a
  prediction term ℓ plus an anti-collapse term Ω that depends on f only through
  the distribution of its embeddings"; "the predictor is the identity"; "Both ℓ
  and Ω are invariant to rotations". -/
  | jepaObjectiveForm
  /-- Idealised world model. Section 2.1: "Let a world be a finite set of states
  z ∈ Z with probabilities p(z) > 0, and let M ≥ 2 modalities observe it,
  modality m through an injective rendering x = g_m(z)". Used by Lemma 1
  (Section A.2: "If g_m is injective ..."). -/
  | finiteWorldInjectiveRenderings
  /-- Every modality is given the same positive pairs. Theorem 1: "share one
  positive-pair distribution, π1 = · · · = πM = π". Section 2.3: "good views fix
  the kernel by design". -/
  | sharedPositivePairDistribution
  /-- Exact minimisers of the population objective over all encoders.
  Section 2.1: "one finite world rendered injectively, shared positives,
  population optima, and minimizers unique up to rotation". Section 7: "Our
  theorems are population statements". -/
  | populationMinimizers
  /-- Good views are built from knowing which texts describe one scene.
  Section 7: "they require knowing which texts describe the same scene, the
  grouping of human captions by image on Flickr and COCO"; "Paired information
  thus enters through the definition of positives". -/
  | positivesGroupedByScene
  /-- The web-text corpus is mostly written by an image captioner. Section 7:
  "on Recap-DataComp two of each image's three texts are generated by an image
  captioner (LLaVA built on LLaMA-3-8B, whose vision encoder is CLIP ViT-L/14,
  34) and carry information distilled from image–text training." Table 7:
  without any image captioner good views reach .060 on WIT against .107. -/
  | captionerWrittenRecapTexts
  /-- The map joining the two encoders is fitted on image–caption pairs.
  Section 1: "One linear map with orthonormal rows, the only part fitted on
  pairs" (30k Flickr pairs, Section 5.1). Forget step offered in the abstract:
  "Even that rotation needs no pair"; Section 5.2 and Table 4 run the tool with
  zero pairs (captioner CIDEr .158 with zero pairs against .430 with the
  30k-pair rotation and .028 for a random map). -/
  | linearMapFittedOnPairs
  /-- The image side is a frozen pretrained DINOv2 (DINOv3 in the captioner).
  Section 2.3: "Our experiments mostly vary the text side against a frozen
  DINOv2"; "our image encoders, and DINOv2, are bad-view JEPAs: their positives
  are crops of one photograph". Figure 1: "alignment with DINOv2". -/
  | frozenDinoImageEncoder
  /-- Pair-free alignment uses an external method as is. Section 5.2: "We test
  it with their method, code, hyperparameters and five seeds unchanged"
  (Schnaus et al. [54]). -/
  | pairFreeMatchingMethod
  /-- The captioner decodes with a pretrained language model. Section 5.1: "with
  a pretrained language model fine-tuned on text alone as the decoder
  (Qwen2.5-1.5B, 49)"; Table 10. -/
  | textOnlyCaptionDecoder
  /-- Both arms draw their texts from Recap-DataComp. Section 4.2: "With the
  same 109M encoder and texts from the same corpus" (the texts of 2.16M images
  for the good-view run, of 940M images for the next-token run). -/
  | sameCorpusBothArms
  /-- The ratio is counted in training samples. Section 4.2: "That is 42 times
  fewer samples, 14 times fewer text tokens since a good-view sample reads three
  texts, and 435 times fewer unique images". -/
  | sampleAsCountingUnit
  /-- Both arms use the 109M BERT-B encoder. Section 4.2: "With the same 109M
  encoder"; for the 33M encoder only a matched-sample gap is given (".108
  against .071 at 21.6M samples"). -/
  | encoder109M
  /-- The two sample counts are the ends of two runs of different scale.
  Section 4.2: "after 21.6M training samples (the texts of 2.16M images), the
  alignment that next-token prediction reaches after 900M samples (.111, the
  texts of 940M images; we scored no earlier next-token checkpoint)". -/
  | runScalesAndEndpoints
  /-- The two arms use different, one-sidedly tuned recipes. Section 4.2: "each
  run uses the recipe of its scale (learning rate 10−4 and batch 256 against
  2 · 10−4 and 2,048)". Section 7: "hyperparameters were tuned only for the
  good-view JEPA". -/
  | recipePerArm
  /-- "Alignment" is one benchmark and one metric. Figure 1: "alignment with
  DINOv2 on the PRH paper's benchmark (WIT-1024, mutual 10-NN)"; Section B, PRH
  protocol: "maximum over layer pairs". Section 4.2 reports that on Flickr the
  same two runs differ ("a higher Flickr R@1 (.388 against .335)"). -/
  | witMutualKnnBenchmark
  /-- The two scores that are called the same. Figure 1: "(.109 and .111 ...)",
  "means of three seeds"; Table 14: next-token seeds .109, .111 and .112. -/
  | measuredWitScores
  /-- Benchmark captions occur in the training texts. Section 4.3: "at least 43
  of the 1,024 WIT captions appear among the Recap-DataComp alt-texts, although
  removing them changes none of the nine text models we checked by more than
  .002" (Section C, "WIT captions in the training texts"; the nine models are
  not itemised). -/
  | witCaptionsInTrainingTexts
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

inductive Hyp where
  /-- Theorem 1: "let the minimizers of Lπ be unique up to rotation".
  Section 2.1: "The key assumption is uniqueness up to rotation, which holds for
  JEPAs with second-order anti-collapse under a spectral gap"; "Proposition 1
  does not cover uniqueness under its full-distribution constraint, which we
  leave as an assumption." -/
  | minimizersUniqueUpToRotation
  /-- Theorem 1's premise π1 = · · · = πM, for trained encoders. Section 2.2:
  "Alas, in practice the modalities' positives are not the same pairs of world
  states." Section 7: "no experiment meets the hypotheses of Theorem 1 exactly,
  since our runs use LeJEPA and the image side mostly uses crops as
  positives". -/
  | positivesSharedAcrossModalities
  /-- Each observation determines its world state. Section 7: "real modalities
  are lossy". Section 2.3: "The model still assumes that every observation
  determines its state, while a caption sees only part of a photograph". -/
  | renderingsInjective
  /-- The trained networks are population minimisers. Section 7: "Our theorems
  are population statements ...; real modalities are lossy, and finite-sample
  training relies on the network's inductive bias." -/
  | populationOptimumReached
  /-- All modalities embed into the same R^k and differ by Q in O(k).
  Section 2.2: "When the two embedding dimensions differ (768 for DINOv2, 384
  for our text JEPA) we fit a linear map with orthonormal rows, a case the
  theory does not cover". -/
  | equalEmbeddingDimensions
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- Section 1, end of the contribution list. The sentence carries one condition,
"with JEPAs". Behind it: Theorem 1 (the "theorem") and Section 5 (the "tool":
captioner and pair-free alignment built on trained encoders, to which the
conclusion "alignment up to one rotation" is applied). Hypothesis statuses are
for that target, the trained encoders of real modalities. -/
def claim1 : Claim Dep Hyp where
  statement :=
    "This demonstrates that the PRH–with JEPAs–is no longer a hypothesis but a theorem and a tool."
  kind := .conditional
  used :=
    [ ⟨.jepaObjectiveForm, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.finiteWorldInjectiveRenderings, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.sharedPositivePairDistribution, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.populationMinimizers, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.positivesGroupedByScene, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.captionerWrittenRecapTexts, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.linearMapFittedOnPairs, .measured⟩,
      ⟨.frozenDinoImageEncoder, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.pairFreeMatchingMethod, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.textOnlyCaptionDecoder, .setByExperimenter⟩ ]
  declared := [.jepaObjectiveForm]
  forgets := [⟨.linearMapFittedOnPairs, .shown⟩]
  hypotheses :=
    [ ⟨.minimizersUniqueUpToRotation, .bet⟩,
      ⟨.positivesSharedAcrossModalities, .knownFalse⟩,
      ⟨.renderingsInjective, .knownFalse⟩,
      ⟨.populationOptimumReached, .bet⟩,
      ⟨.equalEmbeddingDimensions, .knownFalse⟩ ]
  declaredBets := []

#eval audit claim1
#eval verdict claim1

theorem claim1_verdict : verdict claim1 = .rejected := by decide

/-- Theorem 1 as stated (Section 2.1, proof in Section A.2 via Lemma 1). The
statement and its standing definitions carry every premise the proof uses.
Uniqueness up to rotation is an explicit premise that the paper proves only for
whitened second-order anti-collapse under a spectral gap (Proposition 1), so it
is recorded as an openly declared, unchecked hypothesis. -/
def claim2 : Claim Dep Hyp where
  statement :=
    "Let M ≥ 2 modalities share one positive-pair distribution, π1 = · · · = πM = π, and let the minimizers of Lπ be unique up to rotation. Then there is one embedding of the world h⋆ : Z → R^k such that every minimizer f_m⋆ of L_m, for every modality m, satisfies f_m⋆ ∘ g_m = Q_m h⋆ for some Q_m ∈ O(k), so any two modalities agree up to the single rotation."
  kind := .conditional
  used :=
    [ ⟨.jepaObjectiveForm, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.finiteWorldInjectiveRenderings, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.sharedPositivePairDistribution, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.populationMinimizers, .setByExperimenter⟩ ]
  declared :=
    [ .jepaObjectiveForm,
      .finiteWorldInjectiveRenderings,
      .sharedPositivePairDistribution,
      .populationMinimizers ]
  forgets := []
  hypotheses := [⟨.minimizersUniqueUpToRotation, .bet⟩]
  declaredBets := [.minimizersUniqueUpToRotation]

#eval audit claim2
#eval verdict claim2

theorem claim2_verdict : verdict claim2 = .admissibleOnDeclaredBets := by decide

/-- Abstract; derivation in Section 4.2 and Figure 1 (right): one pair of runs,
109M good-view JEPA at 21.6M samples (.109) against 109M next-token prediction
at 900M samples (.111), 900 / 21.6 ≈ 42. The sentence carries "good views",
"training samples", "on the same corpus" and "the same alignment". No theorem is
applied, so there are no hypotheses. The forget step for the captioner-written
texts is Section 7's "the comparisons between views, on identical texts, do
not" rely on them; Table 7 supports the sign of good against bad views without
an image captioner, not the factor 42 against next-token prediction. -/
def claim3 : Claim Dep Hyp where
  statement :=
    "Good views need 42 times fewer training samples than next-token prediction on the same corpus to reach the same alignment"
  kind := .conditional
  used :=
    [ ⟨.positivesGroupedByScene, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.captionerWrittenRecapTexts, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.frozenDinoImageEncoder, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.sameCorpusBothArms, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.sampleAsCountingUnit, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.encoder109M, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.runScalesAndEndpoints, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.recipePerArm, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.witMutualKnnBenchmark, .setByExperimenter⟩,
      ⟨.measuredWitScores, .measured⟩,
      ⟨.witCaptionsInTrainingTexts, .measured⟩ ]
  declared :=
    [ .positivesGroupedByScene,
      .sameCorpusBothArms,
      .sampleAsCountingUnit,
      .measuredWitScores ]
  forgets :=
    [ ⟨.captionerWrittenRecapTexts, .notShown⟩,
      ⟨.witCaptionsInTrainingTexts, .shown⟩ ]
  hypotheses := []
  declaredBets := []

#eval audit claim3
#eval verdict claim3

theorem claim3_verdict : verdict claim3 = .rejected := by decide

end EpistemicLedger.Cases.BlindA.BalestrieroLeCun
